A missing package comes with the command that installs it, on any distribution
==============================================================================

The capability report already says what this session cannot do — no capture, no clipboard, no input
synthesis — and each note names the tool to install. On Arch that name is also the package and
`pacman -S` is the command. Everywhere else the person reading it had to translate.

So the notes carry the command for the family underneath: `pacman -S --needed` on Arch and its children
(Garuda, EndeavourOS, CachyOS, Manjaro, Artix, SteamOS), `apt install` on Debian's (Ubuntu, Mint, Pop,
Kali), `dnf install` on Fedora, `zypper install` on openSUSE — and nothing at all where the family is
unknown, because a wrong command is worse than no command.

Devuan is named separately from Debian even though it resolves to the same packages: telling someone
their Devuan box is Debian reads like the app guessed. LCOS, the Lunduke Computer OS, is Devuan-based
and is recognised by `ID=lcos` as well as by `ID=devuan` and by `ID_LIKE` containing `devuan`.

The BSDs are the reporting half of a port, and are honest about it. Family comes from the target rather
than from a file, because a BSD has no `/etc/os-release`; the package manager is named (`pkg install`,
`pkg_add`, `pkgin`), and every capability hint says there is no packaged equivalent yet instead of
inventing a package name. Nothing here claims a BSD build works — `tao`/`wry` do not target BSD at all,
which is the first wall, not the last.

Detection reads `ID` first and `ID_LIKE` second, which is what makes a derivative resolve without a list
of distro names to maintain. One table holds the package name in each family, keyed on the Arch name the
PKGBUILD already declares.

Six tests: arch/garuda/endeavouros/steamos/artix, debian/ubuntu/mint/pop/kali, devuan/lcos, fedora,
opensuse, gentoo falling through to no hint rather than a wrong one, and the BSD managers.

---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/crates/quixi-platform/src/distro.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,271 @@
+//! Which distribution family this is, and what its package manager calls the thing that is missing.
+//!
+//! The capability report tells a person what the app cannot do; without a package name it leaves them
+//! to translate. "Install ydotool" is an Arch instruction — on Debian the package is `ydotool` too,
+//! but the command is not, and on Fedora and openSUSE half the names differ. Every hint therefore
+//! carries a table row and a command for the family underneath.
+//!
+//! The detection reads `/etc/os-release` once and caches it. `ID_LIKE` is what makes this work on the
+//! derivatives: Garuda, EndeavourOS, CachyOS and Manjaro all say `arch`, Debian's children say
+//! `debian` (Ubuntu, Mint, Pop, Kali), and SteamOS says `arch` before it says anything else.
+
+use std::sync::OnceLock;
+
+#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
+pub enum Family {
+    Arch,
+    Debian,
+    Fedora,
+    Suse,
+    /// Devuan and anything else built on it — the Lunduke Computer OS (LCOS) is one. Debian's package
+    /// vocabulary and `apt`, without systemd. Named separately because a hint that says "Debian" to a
+    /// Devuan user reads like the wrong machine.
+    Devuan,
+    /// BSDs. The package managers differ from every Linux family AND from each other, and the input
+    /// tools this app prefers (ydotool) do not exist there at all, so a hint names the manager rather
+    /// than inventing a package name.
+    FreeBsd,
+    OpenBsd,
+    NetBsd,
+    Unknown,
+}
+
+impl Family {
+    /// The command a person actually types, minus the package list.
+    pub fn command(self) -> Option<&'static str> {
+        match self {
+            Family::Arch => Some("sudo pacman -S --needed"),
+            Family::Debian | Family::Devuan => Some("sudo apt install"),
+            Family::Fedora => Some("sudo dnf install"),
+            Family::Suse => Some("sudo zypper install"),
+            Family::FreeBsd => Some("sudo pkg install"),
+            Family::OpenBsd => Some("doas pkg_add"),
+            Family::NetBsd => Some("pkgin install"),
+            Family::Unknown => None,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// True where the package manager and the tool names do not line up, so a hint must not guess.
+    pub fn is_bsd(self) -> bool {
+        matches!(self, Family::FreeBsd | Family::OpenBsd | Family::NetBsd)
+    }
+
+    pub fn name(self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Family::Arch => "Arch",
+            Family::Debian => "Debian",
+            Family::Fedora => "Fedora",
+            Family::Suse => "openSUSE",
+            Family::Devuan => "Devuan",
+            Family::FreeBsd => "FreeBSD",
+            Family::OpenBsd => "OpenBSD",
+            Family::NetBsd => "NetBSD",
+            Family::Unknown => "unknown",
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// One row per capability the report can name, with the package in each family.
+///
+/// `arch` is the key: the hints are written from the Arch package name because that is what the
+/// PKGBUILD declares, and every other family is a lookup off it.
+struct Pkg {
+    arch: &'static str,
+    debian: &'static str,
+    fedora: &'static str,
+    suse: &'static str,
+}
+
+const TABLE: &[Pkg] = &[
+    Pkg { arch: "ydotool", debian: "ydotool", fedora: "ydotool", suse: "ydotool" },
+    Pkg { arch: "wl-clipboard", debian: "wl-clipboard", fedora: "wl-clipboard", suse: "wl-clipboard" },
+    Pkg { arch: "xclip", debian: "xclip", fedora: "xclip", suse: "xclip" },
+    Pkg { arch: "xsel", debian: "xsel", fedora: "xsel", suse: "xsel" },
+    Pkg { arch: "kdialog", debian: "kdialog", fedora: "kdialog", suse: "kdialog" },
+    Pkg { arch: "zenity", debian: "zenity", fedora: "zenity", suse: "zenity" },
+    Pkg { arch: "imagemagick", debian: "imagemagick", fedora: "ImageMagick", suse: "ImageMagick" },
+    Pkg { arch: "spectacle", debian: "spectacle", fedora: "spectacle", suse: "spectacle" },
+    Pkg { arch: "grim", debian: "grim", fedora: "grim", suse: "grim" },
+    Pkg { arch: "gnome-screenshot", debian: "gnome-screenshot", fedora: "gnome-screenshot", suse: "gnome-screenshot" },
+    Pkg { arch: "xfce4-screenshooter", debian: "xfce4-screenshooter", fedora: "xfce4-screenshooter", suse: "xfce4-screenshooter" },
+    Pkg { arch: "kscreen", debian: "kscreen", fedora: "kscreen", suse: "kscreen" },
+    Pkg { arch: "xorg-xrandr", debian: "x11-xserver-utils", fedora: "xrandr", suse: "xrandr" },
+    Pkg { arch: "wlr-randr", debian: "wlr-randr", fedora: "wlr-randr", suse: "wlr-randr" },
+    Pkg { arch: "xdotool", debian: "xdotool", fedora: "xdotool", suse: "xdotool" },
+    Pkg { arch: "git", debian: "git", fedora: "git", suse: "git" },
+    Pkg { arch: "ghidra", debian: "ghidra", fedora: "ghidra", suse: "ghidra" },
+];
+
+fn parse_os_release(text: &str) -> Family {
+    // ID is authoritative; ID_LIKE is the fallback that catches every derivative.
+    let field = |key: &str| -> Option<String> {
+        text.lines()
+            .find_map(|line| line.strip_prefix(&format!("{key}=")))
+            .map(|v| v.trim().trim_matches('"').to_lowercase())
+    };
+    let classify = |s: &str| -> Option<Family> {
+        if s.contains("arch") || s == "artix" || s == "steamos" {
+            Some(Family::Arch)
+        } else if s.contains("debian") || s.contains("ubuntu") {
+            Some(Family::Debian)
+        } else if s.contains("fedora") || s.contains("rhel") || s.contains("centos") {
+            Some(Family::Fedora)
+        } else if s.contains("suse") {
+            Some(Family::Suse)
+        } else {
+            None
+        }
+    };
+    // Devuan before Debian: its ID_LIKE also says debian, and its ID is the more specific answer.
+    if field("ID").as_deref() == Some("devuan") || field("ID").as_deref() == Some("lcos") {
+        return Family::Devuan;
+    }
+    if let Some(like) = field("ID_LIKE") {
+        if like.split_whitespace().any(|w| w == "devuan") {
+            return Family::Devuan;
+        }
+    }
+    if let Some(id) = field("ID") {
+        if let Some(f) = classify(&id) {
+            return f;
+        }
+    }
+    if let Some(like) = field("ID_LIKE") {
+        if let Some(f) = classify(&like) {
+            return f;
+        }
+    }
+    Family::Unknown
+}
+
+// A BSD binary is built for one of them, so the target decides before any file is read — and on a BSD
+// with no /etc/os-release there is nothing else to ask. `cfg` rather than matching `env::consts::OS`,
+// because that constant is folded at compile time and every arm is then reported unreachable on Linux.
+#[cfg(any(target_os = "freebsd", target_os = "dragonfly"))]
+fn bsd_family() -> Option<Family> { Some(Family::FreeBsd) }
+#[cfg(target_os = "openbsd")]
+fn bsd_family() -> Option<Family> { Some(Family::OpenBsd) }
+#[cfg(target_os = "netbsd")]
+fn bsd_family() -> Option<Family> { Some(Family::NetBsd) }
+#[cfg(not(any(target_os = "freebsd", target_os = "dragonfly", target_os = "openbsd", target_os = "netbsd")))]
+fn bsd_family() -> Option<Family> { None }
+
+pub fn family() -> Family {
+    static CACHE: OnceLock<Family> = OnceLock::new();
+    *CACHE.get_or_init(|| {
+        if let Some(f) = bsd_family() {
+            return f;
+        }
+        std::fs::read_to_string("/etc/os-release")
+            .map(|t| parse_os_release(&t))
+            .unwrap_or(Family::Unknown)
+    })
+}
+
+/// The sentence fragment that turns a package into an instruction on this machine.
+///
+/// Returns an empty string on a family we cannot name a package manager for, so the caller's hint
+/// still reads correctly without inventing `sudo apt install` on a Gentoo box.
+pub fn hint(arch_pkg: &str) -> String {
+    let fam = family();
+    if fam.is_bsd() {
+        return format!(
+            " On {fam}: there is no packaged equivalent yet — see DISTROS.md.", fam = fam.name()
+        );
+    }
+    let Some(command) = fam.command() else { return String::new() };
+    let name = TABLE
+        .iter()
+        .find(|row| row.arch == arch_pkg)
+        .map(|row| match fam {
+            Family::Arch => row.arch,
+            Family::Debian | Family::Devuan => row.debian,
+            Family::Fedora => row.fedora,
+            Family::Suse => row.suse,
+            // Unreachable: both callers return before this on a BSD. Listed so the match is total.
+            Family::FreeBsd | Family::OpenBsd | Family::NetBsd | Family::Unknown => row.arch,
+        })
+        .unwrap_or(arch_pkg);
+    format!(" On {fam}: `{command} {name}`.", fam = fam.name())
+}
+
+/// The same hint for several packages at once — `apt install a b` is one command, not two.
+pub fn hints(arch_pkgs: &[&str]) -> String {
+    let fam = family();
+    if fam.is_bsd() {
+        return format!(
+            " On {fam}: there is no packaged equivalent yet — see DISTROS.md.", fam = fam.name()
+        );
+    }
+    let Some(command) = fam.command() else { return String::new() };
+    let names: Vec<&str> = arch_pkgs
+        .iter()
+        .map(|p| {
+            TABLE
+                .iter()
+                .find(|row| row.arch == *p)
+                .map(|row| match fam {
+                    Family::Arch => row.arch,
+                    Family::Debian | Family::Devuan => row.debian,
+                    Family::Fedora => row.fedora,
+                    Family::Suse => row.suse,
+                    Family::FreeBsd | Family::OpenBsd | Family::NetBsd | Family::Unknown => row.arch,
+                })
+                .unwrap_or(p)
+        })
+        .collect();
+    format!(" On {}: `{} {}`.", fam.name(), command, names.join(" "))
+}
+
+#[cfg(test)]
+mod tests {
+    use super::*;
+
+    #[test]
+    fn arch_and_its_children() {
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=arch\n"), Family::Arch);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=garuda\nID_LIKE=arch\n"), Family::Arch);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=endeavouros\nID_LIKE=arch\n"), Family::Arch);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=steamos\nID_LIKE=arch\n"), Family::Arch);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=artix\n"), Family::Arch);
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn debian_and_its_children() {
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=debian\n"), Family::Debian);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=ubuntu\nID_LIKE=debian\n"), Family::Debian);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=linuxmint\nID_LIKE=\"ubuntu debian\"\n"), Family::Debian);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=pop\nID_LIKE=\"ubuntu debian\"\n"), Family::Debian);
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn fedora_suse_and_the_unknown() {
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=fedora\n"), Family::Fedora);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=opensuse-tumbleweed\nID_LIKE=\"opensuse suse\"\n"), Family::Suse);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=gentoo\n"), Family::Unknown);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release(""), Family::Unknown);
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn devuan_and_lcos_are_their_own_thing() {
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=devuan\nID_LIKE=debian\n"), Family::Devuan);
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=lcos\nID_LIKE=\"devuan debian\"\n"), Family::Devuan);
+        // And anything else Debian-shaped stays Debian.
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=ubuntu\nID_LIKE=debian\n"), Family::Debian);
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn bsd_families_name_a_manager_but_never_a_package() {
+        // family() reads the target for a BSD binary; the hint path is exercised through command().
+        assert_eq!(Family::FreeBsd.command(), Some("sudo pkg install"));
+        assert_eq!(Family::OpenBsd.command(), Some("doas pkg_add"));
+        assert_eq!(Family::NetBsd.command(), Some("pkgin install"));
+        assert!(Family::FreeBsd.is_bsd() && !Family::Arch.is_bsd());
+    }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn id_wins_over_id_like() {
+        // A distro that is Debian-based but calls itself something else still resolves on ID first.
+        assert_eq!(parse_os_release("ID=kali\nID_LIKE=debian\n"), Family::Debian);
+    }
+}
